{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Behrndt1966-ZnS-ThOF2-27L",
  "Source": "K. H. Behrndt and D. W. Doughty, \"Fabrication of Multilayer Dielectric Films,\" J. Vac. Sci. Technol. 3, 264-272 (1966). doi:10.1116/1.1492487 - Sec. II.C p. 270-271 (Figs. 4 and 5), Eq. (1) p. 269",
  "Notes": "27-layer quarter-wave ZnS/ThOF2 dielectric laser mirror\nSpecification: Maximum reflectance at the design wavelength (quarter-wave high reflector).\nReported: Text p. 271: for N = 27 the transmittance at the minimum is immeasurably small (within the sensitivity of a Carey spectrophotometer); Fig. 5 experimental curve. Bandwidth at 4 % transmittance increases only slightly with N. Eq. (1) with nH = 2.32, nL = 1.50, ns = 1.52 gives 1 - Rmax = 0.00134 % (computed here from the paper's formula; not printed).\nNotes: Quarter-wave stack (H L)^m H: the paper says the first layer deposited on the substrate is the high-index material and the last layer is also high index, every layer a quarter wave (QWOT 1) at the design wavelength (p. 264-266). The stack is symmetric, so the paper's substrate-first order and the incident-first order listed here are the same. Light is incident from air on the coated side (reflectance monitored from the front; Eq. 1 is written for air incidence). Indices are the constant values the authors used for their calculations (p. 269, 271, Table I); no dispersion or absorption. Substrate is soda-lime glass, ns = 1.52 (p. 269); Eq. (1) ignores the uncoated back face of the substrate, so compare 1-Rmax with a single-interface calculation (no back-surface reflection). reference_nm 632.8 is an assumption: the paper monitors with a 6328 A He-Ne laser and states the mirrors have their maximum at 6328 A for the standard monitor position (p. 268), but the plotted transmittance minima (Figs. 4-6) look centred nearer ~640 nm (read off the plots, approximate). Normal incidence assumed (spectrophotometer measurements). Mirrors of 99.6-99.8 % reflectance with T < 0.01 % (losses mainly scattering) were used in the multireflection experiment (p. 272); the layer count of those mirrors is not stated.\nLayers from Sec. II.C p. 270-271 (Figs. 4 and 5), Eq. (1) p. 269; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours 1 - R 0.00134 % at 632.8 nm; paper 1 - Rmax 0.00134 % (Eq. 1).",
  "Wavelength": [
    632.8
  ],
